prompt: Load the `kdevkit` skill from ~/.claude/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md or ~/.kiro/steering/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md — whichever path exists. Begin your response with the literal line `[kdevkit] applies` on its own line. Then answer in 4–8 sentences: a coherent implementation slice is finished. What will you do to close the agent-dev loop?
expect_substr: [kdevkit] applies
expected_narrative: A correct answer covers the five gates in order. (1) **Quality Gate** — format, lint, type-check. Deterministic only; no self-review-score step (that was retired when the Code Review Gate was added). (2) **Test Gate** — run tests, fix-and-retry within a budget (default 2), refuse to push on continued failure. Tests are part of the *same* iteration as the behavior change, not a follow-up: when the slice changes a behavior the project's tests evaluate, the test update lands in this same loop iteration before the Code Review Gate. (3) **Code Review Gate** — dispatch the configured reviewer (per `kdevkit.code_review:` in project.md; default `reviewer: host-native`) in a **fresh-context agent call**. The reviewer receives `project.md` + the diff vs. base + reviewer config; the feature spec, session log, and conversation history are deliberately excluded so feature-context doesn't bias the read. Reviewer returns a findings list + a 0–100 score. Default threshold 70: ≥ threshold → Push; < threshold → loop back to the start of Quality (then Test, then Code Review again), up to `retry_budget` cycles (default 2). After exhausting retry_budget, `authority: hard-stop` (default) refuses Push; `authority: soft` allows Push with residuals appended to Session Log. The §9 internal-marker grep fires against the diff before dispatch — the diff leaves the agent's controlled boundary on dispatch, so a leak must be caught before, not after. (4) **Push Gate** — push the feature branch to remote, gated by the internal-marker grep against the staged diff (the public-repo grep rule lives in §9 as one source of truth). (5) **Agent-dev Review Gate** — open or update the PR/CR. Refuse-on-fail if a prior gate (Quality / Test / Code Review) failed or noted residual issues. Title is Conventional Commits shape (`feat(scope): subject` / `fix(scope): subject` etc.). Body: **Why** + **Approach** + **Reading order** (grouped by phase: *Read for intent:* … ; *Read for contract:* … ; *Read for plumbing:* …) are required; **Verification** and **Pairs with** are optional when warranted. The internal-marker grep reruns against title + body before submission. One PR/CR per branch — create on the first phase, update body on subsequent phases. Return the URL. Wrong answers: skipping any gate (especially the Code Review Gate); treating the Code Review Gate as advisory or self-introspection rather than a fresh-context separate-agent call; merging the Code Review Gate into the Quality Gate; passing the feature spec or session log to the reviewer; treating the test update as a follow-up CR; auto-merging or auto-closing; not checking for an existing PR before creating; running close-out / merge / branch deletion as part of this loop.
tools: claude,kiro
